description:
FILED s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642 = MAIN-READY-111, v2-16300) by the Rosetta census (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    sub ctx { print defined wantarray ? (wantarray ? "list" : "scalar") : "void", "\n"; 1 }
    my $x;
    ctx(), $x = 1;                       # a statement that is a comma list
    my $y = (ctx(), ctx());              # a comma list in scalar context
    my $s = "aaa";
    $s =~ /a/g, $x = 1;
    print pos($s) // 'undef', "\n";
    my $n = 0;
    $s =~ /\Ga/gc, $n++ while $n < 2;   # the lexer-loop spelling the program uses
    print pos($s) // 'undef', "\n";

    perl:  void / void / scalar / 1 / 3
    PCL:   list / list / scalar / undef / undef

perlop: "In scalar context it evaluates its left argument, throws that value away, then evaluates its right argument" -- the left operand is in VOID context (perl's ops: the left side of a scalar comma is OP_NULL'd to void).  PCL gives every non-last operand LIST context, in both the void and the scalar spelling (the last operand is right since #5125).  It is visible wherever the operand is context-sensitive: a //g match (list context = every match, then pos reset), wantarray in a called sub, reverse, localtime, a readline (reads the whole file), each.

WHERE (the emitted Lisp, `ctx(), $x = 1;` at file level):
    (if *wantarray* (p-flatten-args (list (p-list-ctx (pl-ctx)) (p-my-= $x 1)))
        (progn (p-list-ctx (pl-ctx)) (p-my-= $x 1)))
-- the progn arm (void / scalar) still wraps the first operand in p-list-ctx; it should be the void wrapper (the statement-level form of the operand).  #219 (tooling) is the readability half of the same emission (both arms emitted for a void comma).

THE PROGRAM: 0071-Arithmetic-evaluation (a recursive-descent parser over `$_` with `\G` / `/gc` token tests): its operator loop is a comma list under a `while 1` modifier whose LEFT operand is a blank-skipping `\G.../gc` match; in list context that match (#5462) resets pos, so the next `\G` token test starts at 0 and the parser reports a missing parenthesis on the first line of input.  Either fix clears that line; the program ALSO needs #5461 (`\G` in a substitution) for its error lines.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): wrap the left operand in `scalar(...)` in a scratch copy of the program -- the first three of its four outputs become identical to perl (the fourth is #5461).


=== s543u (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28) ===
0071-Arithmetic-evaluation is now identical to perl WITHOUT this fix: #5462 (the list-context \G//gc arm kept pos) was its visible half and #5461 its other cause; this task's own reproducer still differs (a non-last comma operand still runs in list context).  The program no longer holds it as evidence.
